    {
      "id": "K52G90jvCVI84_x2Gf9Tq",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1173.5695419921876,
      "y": 767,
      "width": 472.39501953125,
      "height": 615.4000000000005,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aw",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1516403615,
      "version": 969,
      "versionNonce": 1611729553,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1763840408025,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "we can create a bean using @Configuration\n\nand this is actually one of the official ways to\ndefine Spring beans.\n\n1. What is @Configuration?\n\n@Configuration tells Spring:\n“This class contains methods that create beans.”\nIt is like a factory class for beans.\n\n\n@Configuration\npublic class AppConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    public Car car() {\n        return new Car();   // Spring creates this object as a bean\n    }\n}\n\n\nHere:\n\n@Configuration → tells Spring this class has bean definitions\n@Bean → tells Spring that this method returns a bean\nnew Car() → Spring uses this object and stores it in the container\n\n\n\nThis is often used when:\nYou cannot put @Component on a class\nYou want custom building logic\nYou want more control over the object creation",
      "fontSize": 14.480000000000013,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "we can create a bean using @Configuration\n\nand this is actually one of the official ways to\ndefine Spring beans.\n\n1. What is @Configuration?\n\n@Configuration tells Spring:\n“This class contains methods that create beans.”\nIt is like a factory class for beans.\n\n\n@Configuration\npublic class AppConfig {\n\n    @Bean\n    public Car car() {\n        return new Car();   // Spring creates this object as a bean\n    }\n}\n\n\nHere:\n\n@Configuration → tells Spring this class has bean definitions\n@Bean → tells Spring that this method returns a bean\nnew Car() → Spring uses this object and stores it in the container\n\n\n\nThis is often used when:\nYou cannot put @Component on a class\nYou want custom building logic\nYou want more control over the object creation",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
